::STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A teacher records each students quiz score. Which display could show the distribution?
{
=A. Dot plot#Correct. A dot plot can show one numerical variable and preserve individual values.
~B. Two-way table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables.
~C. Pie chart#A pie chart is for categorical proportions.
~D. Segmented bar graph#A segmented bar graph compares category proportions across groups.
}

::STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A bar chart shows favorite project type with the tallest bar at video. What conclusion is supported?
{
~A. Video caused higher grades#A bar chart of preferences does not prove cause.
=B. Video was chosen by the greatest number of students in the display#Correct. The tallest category bar indicates the greatest count.
~C. Every student chose video#Other bars may also have responses.
~D. Video is a numerical variable#Project type is categorical.
}

::STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A segmented bar graph shows Grade 10 has a larger car segment than Grade 9. What is supported?
{
~A. Grade 10 must have more total students#Segmented bars compare proportions, not necessarily totals.
~B. Car choice caused grade level#The graph does not show causation.
=C. Grade 10 has a greater proportion choosing car#Correct. Segment size represents relative frequency within each grade.
~D. No Grade 9 students chose car#A smaller segment may still be greater than zero.
}

::STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display fits grade level and preferred study location?
{
~A. Histogram#Histograms show one numerical variable.
~B. Scatterplot#Scatterplots require two numerical variables.
~C. Box plot#Box plots summarize numerical distributions.
=D. Two-way table#Correct. Both variables are categorical, so a two-way table organizes category combinations.
}

::STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L08_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
What should be checked before interpreting any data display?
{
=A. Title, labels, units, scale, and context#Correct. These components explain what quantities and categories mean.
~B. Only the color of the graph#Color alone does not define the data meaning.
~C. Only the first answer choice#Assessment choices are not graph components.
~D. Only whether the display is large#Size does not guarantee correct interpretation.
}

